ood at dealing with  students but I was surprised that the  sheer volume of this and that also  started coming up with some of the other  videos that I was doing as well now I  left Fayetteville State University after  organizing university-wide assessment  and helping them write their tenure  quality enhancement plan being up for  early tenure and promotion I left all  that behind it was a whole story about  that that perhaps I'll do a video on  some other time and I moved up to New  York essentially because we were doing  this long distance relationship and  going from North Carolina to New York  was was quite a drag so now we were we  were together you know in one place Andy  and I and I started teaching for Marist  College and I did the same thing there I  started recording my lectures but now I  uploaded them into my youtube channel in  part because you could actually upload  longer videos and in part because you  know Maris wasn't like Fayetteville  State University asserting you know  intellectual property rights over the  stuff that I produced it was my  intellectual property and now I was no  longer teaching critical thinking I was  teaching things that had a bit more  substance you know intro to philosophy  and ethics and I have my students read  primary text so we're reading Plato and  Aristotle and Thomas Hobbes and  Descartes and you know going on and on  and on so so as recording stuff no  longer just from a textbook but from the  classics and those caught on very  quickly as well and people started  saying the same sort of comments you  know my professor is not explaining this  to me I'm not understanding the text  your videos are helping me out with this  a lot of other people started saying  things like I've been out of college for  a long time and I wished I could go back  i watch your videos because it gives me  the feeling of being in a classroom or I  can't afford to go to college so I'm  using your videos as a resource and  eventually it got to the point where you  know not only were students and lifelong  learners using them but also other  professors were you know watching my  videos to sometimes get ideas about how  to teach the material or see whether  they were on the right track or  sometimes just for enjoyment and then I  started shooting other videos and other  sequences outside of the classroom you  notice that chalkboard behind me and  those that lighting stuff those are  investments that I made we made rather  over time after we determined that I  should no longer simply rely on being  able to find an open class room to shoot  a video or have a class on it we should  actually take control over the video  production process and that is in fact  what we have been doing I also now  recently acquired a voice recorder with  a lapel mic that that we use as well and  I started you know shooting other series  like the existentialist series right  existentialist philosophy and literature  or one-offs like on plato's ion or on  Humes you know Natural History of  religion things that I hadn't talked  about in class and then I started  shooting core concept videos use much  shorter you know 10 to 25 minute videos  focused on one particular issue or  distinction or or argument in a  philosophical text and those have been  you know incredibly helpful for people  as well and then I started the half-hour  Hagel series and I started doing other  things like spinning off additional  channels like a critical thinking  argumentation and logic channel or a  history of ideas channel and I've use  the videos as the core for other larger  things like online classes so you know  I've been engaging in this for a law  time and it's grown and it's grown in  its growth because people you know do  like the kind of work that I'm doing and  I you know once again I have to say that  I would not have really thought of doing  this myself had it not been for my at  that time fiance now wife suggesting it  and then suggesting it's more and  suggesting it some more and sticking  with it so that's that's some some  background stuff some back story and I  think viewers might be interested now  let's talk about my answers to these  questions why do I engage in philosophy  on YouTube so answer number one is it's  possible you know the Internet has  become in some respects a great  equalizer when it comes to you know  breaking in in other respects of course  the big players are the big players may  have many more resources than than we do  but the sort of thing that I have been  doing wouldn't have been possible say 20  30 years ago where you needed a lot of  you know studio stuff to do video  production and you know it was really  confined to whoever was interested in  the tech or to prestige projects that  were put about you know you think about  things that have been around for a while  like like the great courses for example  they invite in people who they think are  going to be big drawers and they throw a  lot of money at stuff you know some  other things like Khan Academy started  out very small on YouTube and then other  people were like this is great let's  throw some money at them and they  suddenly became very big there's there's  other institutions like say for example  the Sailor Foundation who use other  people's materials and sometimes produce  a bit of their own they've used some of  mine for example there existentialism  classes for me you know the idea that if  I want to for my students first  all produce resources that I have  control over and I can decide you know  what goes where at what time what's  going to be most helpful for for them is  incredibly liberating and the idea as  well that if I do good work people are  going to share it and notice it and you  know talk about it there's something  there as well and so the internet makes  this this sort of thing possible the  second answer to this question why do I  engage in philosophy on YouTube is it's  needed  it really is needed it's surprising  because we have a whole world of people  out there who could be producing content  for YouTube many of them are you know in  on this particular philosopher more  competent than I am I'm sort of a  generalist you know I have my interests  extended into many different people  which means that I don't you know get to  study quite as much Hegel as somebody  who is specializing as a Hegel scholar  and doesn't have to you know do all this  other stuff on the side and may not  actually know all that much about the  other stuff as well be in part because  they don't want to but here's the thing  very few people are in fact doing this  what's needed is people to produce  content that becomes publicly available  on these you know philosophers these  texts there's certain aspects of it that  are really important you gotta actually  know what you're talking about so that  eliminates some of the people who shoot  the videos and you know there's there's  others who don't shoot the videos who  could be doing it you also have to be  able to be on camera right and many  people get very nervous about that it's  probably as scary as public speaking is  for many people strangely for professors  you know who are okay at being in a  classroom you think they would be okay  with being on video but many of them are  there are huge huge gaps even today in  the materials that are available on even  quite important philosophers and I am  doing my best to remedy these gaps  shooting on average now about 250 videos  per year  you know I concentrate on certain  figures so I have a lot of material for  example on Aristotle's Nicomachean  ethics there isn't that much more that  needs to be done on mantle there's a few  places where I could say a bit more  about it I'm just finishing up a series  on Aristotle's categories I've got you  know quite a bit of stuff out there on  Epictetus as a stoic that there's other  Stoics that I should be concentrating on  as well because there's so few people  shooting video and you know there's  plenty of people shooting you know five  to ten minute clips with lots of glitz  and stuff like that but there's very  little substance to those those leave  people rather hungry and this goes to  the other part of it's needed there is  an incredible hunger out there right now  for substantive engagement with ideas  it's not being satisfied by our academic  institutions when it comes to the  general public it's not being satisfied  by our media including educational  channels it's not being satisfied by the  you know high production let's do a five  minute clip on Descartes stuff either so  you know there's a real need for for the  kind of work that that I'm doing and  over time we're building a body of work  think about the half hour Hegel series  when I may actually finish with that in  another two years hopefully there will  be a video commentary consisting of  about 300 videos that leads think the  leads anybody who wants to study Hegel  through one of the most difficult works  of Western philosophy the phenomenology  of spirit let's talk about the third  answer which is that I am a good fit I  didn't realize that as it turns out one  of the talents that I have  and I'll talk a little bit about how  this happens because the case is taking  complex philosophical ideas thinkers tax  and talking about them in such a way  that that non academics and non  philosophy majors can learn about them  can come away with a solid understanding  without watering the stuff down it's  very very difficult to do that  apparently for many people because  they're great at watering the stuff down  and then it's watered down or they talk  at a very high level of theory without a  lot of examples and analogies and  relating it or perhaps they talk in such  a way that they just you know turn off  the viewers so there's it's hard to get  those two together and as it turns out  that is one of the things that I'm  particularly versed in and that came  about not because of any I think great  talent on my part but by putting in year  after year after year of teaching what  we call service classes that's critical  thinking intro to philosophy ethics  philosophy of human nature these these  very basic level classes that we teach  to non majors those are some of the  hardest classes to teach they're the the  least compensated for um and so there's  very little prestige but if you do them  well you have the opportunity to reach  far more people than in any other venue  that you might be in so that's that's  that was the the apprenticeship that  went through that's where I learned my  my craft and as it turns out I you know  I mentioned that the fear that people  have about being on camera I don't have  that fear for whatever reason I never  did my main worry is about whether I'm  expressing myself well so sometimes I'll  let you in on a little secret at the  beginning of videos I may do a number of  takes where I get you know a few  sentences into it or a minute into it  and I'm like this is no good I got to  start again right I did that actually in  this video but once I get rolling  I really don't have any any problem  keeping keeping the discourse going in  part because I'm explaining stuff that  that you know matters to me so all of  this makes me a particularly good fit  for being able to fill in these these  gaps that I'm saying are out there and  satisfy this demand that that's there  for substantive engagement with ideas  for talking about philosophy without  talking down to people so that is the  third thing let me talk about the the  fourth answer to why it is that I engage  in philosophy on YouTube and that is  quite simply that there is a lot of  enjoyment and satisfaction on my own  part and this fits in a little bit with  the first reason about it's possible  right I you know I'm somebody who has  engaged in traditional academic  publication you know I used to write  anywhere between two to three academic  articles year I've written a book which  is actually a set of translations with a  huge hundred page introduction and  massive chronological bibliography isn't  about a debate that took place in the  1930s in France I I did my time as an  academic and I enjoy doing that sort of  thing and I also do enjoy going around  and doing public speaking and I also do  enjoy the other activities that I engage  in outside of the Academy doing the  consulting work as a you know  philosopher for instance with ethics  consulting doing the public speaking  doing the you know tutorials and the  philosophical counseling and coaching I  love doing all of that stuff but I also  love shooting these videos I love being  able to go to the channel and look and  see that I have actually covered all of  Aristotle's Nicomachean ethics or you  know that I'm 180 video installations  into the half-hour Hegel series and that  you know I'm close you know two years  away from being able to provide an  entire commentary in video form on this  this  work that at this point you know tens of  thousands of people have watched and  used for for their own study of Hegel  there's an incredible satisfaction that  comes from knowing that I am providing a  service that you know people really do  appreciate and that's needed that's  particularly needed in our times as we  you know move into this new millennium  and we're working with new media this is  this is absolutely vital as far as I'm  concerned and I try to bring other  people into it you know I give coaching  advice quite often to other people who  are trying to do the same work that I'm  doing whether they're in academia or  outside of academia so there's there's a  lot of satisfaction there that helps to  sustain me the fifth answer to why it is  that I engage in philosophy on YouTube  is that it does also help me to earn my  living now the YouTube itself does not  pay for very much quite frankly you know  the advertisements on on videos at one  time we're paying much more to us  YouTube creators than they currently do  right now you know it's a pretty  surprising how little it actually  provide and that's just life but what I  found is that people watch the videos  and they say well I like the way this  guy teaches so maybe I should take an  online class with them or if they have  the resources or they know other people  who do they say I'd love to hear this  guy speak let's bring him in to do a  talk or they they say you know this guy  seems like somebody I'd like to work  with all hired him as a tutor or as a  philosophical counselor or coach to work  with me on you know learning something  or on issues or you know people contact  me for consulting gigs and that's that's  a very important aspect I should also  mention that I am going to be  creating a patreon page for this this  YouTube and everything else that I'm  doing as well  because people have been asking for that  it seems like a really great idea to me  now let me talk now that I've gone  through those five answers right and I  told you a little bit about the  backstory let me tell you a little bit  about the plans because people are also  you know sometimes asking about this and  sometimes expressing worries I have no  plans to get off of YouTube anytime that  I can think of as a matter of fact yes  if YouTube remains more or less the same  sort of thing I mean who knows what it's  going to look like 20 years from now but  let's assume that things remain more or  less equal I can see myself still  producing content 20 years from now not  only because like I said I do actually  enjoy it  or it's possible but also because I  think by that time there's still going  to be so many thinkers and so many texts  that have not been satisfactorily  discussed and and maybe I'll be able to  do stuff at a higher level around that  time as well you know discussing things  that I didn't get to in the past people  are saying well okay you're presenting  me - what's your criticism of Nietzsche  well I don't have time to talk about  that right now right or you know how do  these these different thinkers connect  with each other there is so much that  can be worked on with with YouTube that  I you know I think I found one of my  callings in life which is producing  these videos so that that is what I've  got lined up you know some of the things  that I have in my mind for going on in  the future are you know doing a series  for example on political philosophy  that's something I'd love to do love to  get back and finally finish up that  existentialist philosophy and literature  series there are literally thousands of  core concept videos that I I would like  to produce  I want to use this half-hour hagel  methodology with you know first reading  the text and having the text available  on the screen and then commenting on it  portion my portion to all sorts of other  texts ranging from you know Cicero to  you know Aristotle's other works to Rene  Descartes to you know everybody else  quite frankly you know there's more than  enough work for for me to do for the  foreseeable future the only question is  it has to be sustainable for me in the  way that I actually earn my living  so that that's the problem probably the  main restriction on things how much time  do I have to spend doing other things  that are living as opposed to shooting  videos that unless you know people start  you know supporting or something like  that aren't quickly earning much of it  but I really love doing this I think  that it's a valuable thing for me to be  engaged in influence a lot of really  great discussions and relationships and  even some some friendships worldwide  with people who I otherwise would not  have come into contact with so you know  that's where all closed thinking about  how wonderful it is that we live in a  time where just you know looking at your  phone you can learn about Plato or learn  about st. Anselm or learn about Hegel  and if you want you can actually say  something in your response so that's  where we'll leave off
 

